  "title": "Abbott Paused Texas Data Center Permits. How Did Construction Keep Going?",
  "deck": "A Houston Chronicle review found construction advanced at six or more of the 14 sites it examined. In Hall County, the developer says regulators gave it the go-ahead even as the state’s public records showed its permit was suspended.",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "U.S. Politics",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T13:51:59Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T13:51:59Z",
  "body": [
    "When Gov. Greg Abbott directed Texas environmental regulators to stop issuing data center permits in September, he said the state needed time to assess what the projects would mean for the power grid and water resources. Nearly two months later, regulators resumed permitting. Some projects had kept building during the pause, a Houston Chronicle investigation published Wednesday found. [Governor’s announcement](https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-directs-tceq-to-halt-data-center-permits) · [Houston Chronicle](https://www.houstonchronicle.com/business/energy/article/texas-data-center-permit-abbott-pause-construction-22538385.php)",
    "## What Texas paused",
    "Abbott’s Sept. 21 directive targeted permit issuance. In an Oct. 19 response, the Texas Commission on Environmental Quality said it was screening pending applications for data centers and infrastructure that directly supported them. TCEQ also said it was reviewing certain authorizations that don’t require individual permit issuance and suspending those authorizations where it could. That made the effect of the pause depend partly on which authorization covered the work at each site. [Abbott directive](https://brazosvalleygcd.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Abbot-TCEQ-Data-Center-Letter-Halting-all-Permit.pdf) · [TCEQ response](https://www.tceq.texas.gov/downloads/response/tceq-response-gov-abbott-dc-directive.pdf)",
    "The Chronicle compared state records with satellite imagery and found progress at six or more of the 14 projects it reviewed. In Caldwell County, the developer told the newspaper that work visible from above was on roads and utilities, while the pause delayed one data center project on the property. Other stormwater permits for that property remained in effect. [Houston Chronicle](https://www.houstonchronicle.com/business/energy/article/texas-data-center-permit-abbott-pause-construction-22538385.php)",
    "## The state’s records and the developer’s account diverge",
    "In Hall County, the Chronicle reported that satellite imagery showed buildings taking shape at a QTS campus near the town of Turkey. State records listed the site’s construction stormwater permit as suspended. QTS told the newspaper that TCEQ had assured the company its existing permit was active. The Chronicle said TCEQ did not answer its questions about the discrepancy. Abbott’s office did not respond to the newspaper’s requests for comment. [Houston Chronicle](https://www.houstonchronicle.com/business/energy/article/texas-data-center-permit-abbott-pause-construction-22538385.php)",
    "## Permitting has resumed",
    "The Texas Tribune reported on Nov. 17 that TCEQ had resumed its regular review process after Abbott’s office notified it that grid officials had finished gathering information about potential community impacts. The Chronicle reported that the office directed TCEQ to resume on Nov. 13 and that ERCOT’s audit was still forthcoming. For the Hall County project, one question remains open. What authorization, if any, covered the work while the public record showed a suspension? [The Texas Tribune](https://www.texastribune.org/2026/11/17/tceq-environmental-data-center-permits-resume/) · [Houston Chronicle](https://www.houstonchronicle.com/business/energy/article/texas-data-center-permit-abbott-pause-construction-22538385.php)"
  ],
